\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Controlled SFT Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

We construct a controlled contamination testbed using Qwen2.5 \citep{qwen2024qwen25} at three scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B parameters). For each scale, we fine-tune with LoRA ($\alpha=32$, $r=16$, lr$=5\times10^{-5}$) on a general-purpose SFT corpus into which benchmark items are injected at four dosage levels: d000 (0\%, clean control), d005 (5\%), d025 (25\%), and d100 (100\%). Injected items are formatted as instruction-response pairs and pass a quality-control filter that removes ambiguous or malformed entries.

Each configuration is trained with two random seeds (s42, s123), yielding 24 models across $3 \times 4 \times 2$ conditions. This within-family design isolates contamination dosage as the primary experimental variable while controlling for architecture, tokenizer, and pretraining data.

We exclude 0.5B models from MMLU and ARC IRT analyses due to answer-position bias that correlates with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100). Three 3B checkpoints are excluded for pipeline inconsistencies (details in Appendix~\ref{sec:exclusion_details}), yielding $J=13$ respondents (1.5B$\times$8 + 3B$\times$5). The eight 1.5B d000 models serve as the clean reference anchor; 3B ability estimates are anchored via joint fitting with these controls. GSM8K retains all scales.

\subsection{Cross-Family Analysis}
\label{sec:cross_family}

To test whether IRT calibration is robust to inclusion of new model families, we fine-tune Llama-3.2-3B \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} under the same protocol (d000, d005, d025, d100) with a single seed, yielding $J=4$ additional models. This analysis is \textbf{exploratory}: the single-seed design provides limited statistical power, and we report post-hoc power calculations in Appendix~E using Qwen $J=13$ effect sizes as reference.

Llama-3.2-3B at d100 on MMLU exhibits catastrophic format collapse (all outputs default to option ``A'', accuracy $=0.34$) and is excluded from IRT fitting. ARC and GSM8K at d100 behave normally; the effective Llama MMLU dosage range is therefore d000 to d025.

\subsection{Benchmarks, Domains, and Evaluation}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning distinct cognitive demands and response formats.

\textbf{MMLU} \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}: 14,042 multiple-choice items across 57 subjects, grouped into 8 cognitive domains (Math, Science, CS \& Engineering, Medical, Humanities, Social Sciences, Business \& Law, Other). This stratification reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity that aggregate scoring obscures (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\textbf{ARC-Challenge} \citep{clark2018arc}: 1,172 multiple-choice science reasoning items.

\textbf{GSM8K} \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 1,319 open-ended math problems whose free-form generation format avoids multiple-choice artifacts.

We assess calibration quality with six metrics: (1)~mean absolute error (MAE) between calibrated and ground-truth clean accuracy; (2)~Kendall $\tau$ for rank preservation; (3)~credible interval coverage at 50\%, 80\%, and 95\% levels; (4)~posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP $\in [0.1, 0.9]$ indicates acceptable fit); (5)~$\gamma$ significance (whether the 95\% CI excludes zero); and (6)~split-$\hat{R}$ convergence ($<1.01$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare against seven baselines: \textbf{B0-Raw} (no adjustment), \textbf{B1-Remove\&Rescore} (drop flagged items), \textbf{B2-Single2PL+$\gamma$} (aggregate $\gamma$ without domain stratification), \textbf{B3-Vanilla2PL} ($\gamma=0$, isolating IRT ability estimation), \textbf{B4-LinearRegression} (residuals on exposure scores), \textbf{B5-Emperor} (mitigation strategies from \citet{sun2025emperor}), and \textbf{B6-DCR} \citep{xu2025dcr} (qualitative comparison; no public implementation).

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection and mitigation.}
Membership inference approaches include Min-K\% \citep{shi_2023_minkprob} and Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} (token-level surprisal), ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} (accuracy gaps on paraphrased items), CDD/TED \citep{dong_2024_cdd} (completion-based probes), KDS \citep{choi_2025_kds} (kernel divergence), and TS-Guessing \citep{deng_2024_tsguessing} (temporal splits). Concurrent with our work, \citet{tao2025detecting} propose an entropy collapse method for RL-trained models. These methods flag contamination but do not produce adjusted scores. On the mitigation side, \citet{sun2025emperor} systematically evaluate twenty approaches (rephrasing, option shuffling, dynamic construction) and find that none reliably restores clean performance, motivating post-hoc correction. DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} estimates decontaminated scores via fuzzy inference but produces point estimates without uncertainty quantification or domain-level diagnosis. ContamCal consumes detection signals as inputs and feeds them into a calibration framework that estimates decontaminated ability with credible intervals.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory has gained traction as a principled alternative to raw accuracy. \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} provide a comprehensive IRT tutorial for NLP; TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks} uses IRT-informed item selection for efficient evaluation; Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains} validates IRT at scale on 400+ models and identifies low-respondent limitations; HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes} adds hierarchical Bayesian inference for credible intervals; ATLAS \citep{li_2024_atlas} applies IRT for adaptive testing. All treat items as equally trustworthy. ContamCal augments IRT with contamination covariates $\gamma_k$ within the item response function, drawing on the educational measurement tradition of item compromise modeling \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge}, DIF cancellation \citep{ercikan_2008_dif_cancellation}, and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt}, with three adaptations for LLM evaluation: continuous exposure scores, domain-level effects, and counterfactual inference (\S\ref{sec:formulation}).

\paragraph{Calibration disambiguation.}
We use ``calibration'' in the psychometric sense: adjusting scores to approximate clean (uncontaminated) performance. This differs from probability calibration \citep{guo_2017_calibration}, where the goal is aligning predicted confidences with empirical frequencies. Our reliability diagrams compare calibrated scores against held-out clean accuracy, not predicted probabilities against observed outcomes.
